 "body": "## What\n\nRe-synchronizes the substrate-generated projections that `python3 -m learner.substrate --check` flagged as DRIFT on `main` @ `6d72735b` (AID-396):\n\n- `engines/minimaxDojo/whiteboard/{profile.yaml,learner_profile.md,trail.md}`\n- `engines/voxelDojo/shared/content.ts`\n\n## Root cause\n\n1. **voxelDojo `shared/content.ts` was genuinely stale**: the canonical learner state gained a verified gate outcome on 2026-08-13 (`lastGateDate`, `longest: 1 -> 2`) that was never projected, and the file predated the current pinned clock (`as_of: 2026-08-21`: `overdue 27d -> 43d`, one unit `today -> overdue 4d`).\n2. **Whiteboard `updated`/`atualizado` stamps still used `datetime.date.today()`** (`adapters/whiteboard.py:32,104`) instead of the pinned projection clock, so `--check` drifted again every calendar day after the last sync. This PR sources them from `projection_today()`, matching the byte-stability invariant stated in `projection_clock.py`.\n\n## Scope / invariants kept\n\n- Canonical `learner/learning_state.yaml`: **unchanged** (no learner-state change).\n- No hand-edits to generated projections: everything regenerated via the canonical flow (`python3 -m learner.substrate`).\n- `projection_clock.yaml as_of` deliberately kept at `2026-08-21` (no bump) so `learner.ts`, `today.ts`, and `reviewSlice.ts` stay byte-stable \u2014 smallest possible ground movement.\n- New regression test in `tests/test_projection_clock.py` asserts whiteboard stamps come from the pinned clock.\n\n## Evidence\n\n- `python3 -m learner.substrate --check` \u2192 exit 0 (`Canonical learner state and generated projections are in sync.`)\n- `python3 -m unittest discover -s learner/substrate/tests` \u2192 **195/195 OK** (incl. new clock test)\n\n## Sequencing note (merge gate)\n\nAID-396 requires this to land **after QA AID-325** (post-cohort regression matrix on PR #182) completes, to avoid moving the ground under the running matrix. Please **hold merge until AID-325 is closed**; the PR is otherwise complete and green.\n\nCloses AID-396 (board: Paperclip).",
